# Client guard (server/README.md "Client guard"): only the official Cinders of Chaos client may play. Owner request:
# "block anyone from playing with clients that are not ours". No client check is unbreakable (the key is inside every
# client binary, only obfuscated, and the protocol can be proxied); this stops stock and third-party clients and casually
# modified ones and raises the bar for the rest. The client half is client/base-patches/0035-client-guard.patch.
#   * login gate (protocollogin.cpp, protocolgame.cpp): the u32 "client version" field both login packets already carry
#     (it was skipped) must be 0x4343 << 16 | build, with build in clientGuardBuilds (empty: any) and not below the
#     min_package of the updater manifest clientGuardManifest (when set). Stock clients send 1098 there. Refused with
#     "Please use the official Cinders of Chaos client: <clientGuardDownloadUrl>" (account login: before the password
#     check; game login: before the character is loaded). With the guard on, a client of another protocol version gets
#     the same message instead of "Only clients with protocol 10.98 allowed!".
#   * handshake (protocolgame.cpp): right after the game login, before the login packets, the server sends a 16-byte
#     random nonce on extended opcode 189 ("cg1:<hex>"); the client answers from C++ with "cg1:<build>:<hex>" of
#     HMAC-SHA256(key, nonce || u16le #account || account || u16le #character || character || u16le build) (Crypto++,
#     constant-time compare). A wrong or missing answer (clientGuardTimeout seconds, default 10) is logged and, in
#     "enforce", the player gets the same message and is logged out (Player::kickPlayer, so logout scripts run). Every
#     other extended opcode the client sends before its answer is held back (at most 64) and handed to Lua after it,
#     so no module loses data; after a failure they are dropped.
#   * keys: config.lua clientGuardKey, else the file clientGuardKeyFile, else the environment variable CHAOS_CLIENT_KEY;
#     64 hex digits each, several (comma separated) during a key rotation. The public development key
#     (ClientGuard::DEV_KEY_HEX, what a client built without the secret gets) is refused unless
#     clientGuardAllowDevKey = true; an answer made with it is named in the log.
#   * clientGuardMode: "off" (the default: an older config.lua changes nothing), "log" (every check runs and is logged,
#     nobody is refused or kicked) or "enforce" (anything else counts as "enforce"). Staff: accounts of type gamemaster or
#     higher logging in from an IP in clientGuardStaffIps skip both checks (nobody is exempt by default).
#   * log: one line per refusal / kick / log-only pass on the console and in clientGuardLogFile (data/logs/clientguard.log).
#   * Lua: player:getClientGuard() -> {mode, state, official, build}; player:clientGuardFail(reason) (used by
#     data/scripts/chaos/clientguard.lua for its module hello check: logged, kicked in "enforce").
#   * startup: mode, key count and source, builds and an HMAC self-test on the console; an error line when enforcing
#     without a usable key (then every handshake fails: fail closed).
# New src/clientguard.cpp / .h (listed in src/CMakeLists.txt; a Visual Studio project needs them added by hand).
# Apply in the server source root after 0001-0020: patch -p1 < 0021-client-guard.patch, then rebuild.
